n\n**Key features:**\n- Uses `asyncio.Semaphore` to limit the number of concurrent tasks\n- Each task acquires the semaphore before running, then releases it when done\n- `asyncio.gather()` runs all the wrapped tasks concurrently\n- **Interrupt handling**: When you press Ctrl+C, the KeyboardInterrupt propagates, `gather()` cancels pending tasks, but their cleanup code (finally blocks, context manager exits) still runs because asyncio properly finalizes cancelled tasks before re-raising the exception\n\nYou can test it like this:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom run import run_tasks\n\nasync def sample_task(n):\n    try:\n        print(f\"Task {n} starting\")\n        await asyncio.sleep(2)\n        print(f\"Task {n} done\")\n    finally:\n        print(f\"Task {n} cleanup\")\n\nasyncio.run(run_tasks(\n    [lambda: sample_task(i) for i in range(10)],\n    max_concurrent=3\n))\n```\n\nIf you Ctrl+C during execution, you'll see all the cleanup code runs for both completed and interrupted tasks.","ttft_ms":11074,"type":"result","duration_ms":14534,"uuid":"691c1fb3-fc24-467f-9f6e-c2e9b632c568","ttft_stream_ms":1982,"time_to_request_ms":72,"queued_turn_count":0}
